fix(deploy): read the log's pin from the repository, and refuse a silent mint (#242)

The transparency log's tree id is pinned in a gitignored file, so a git worktree
is a checkout that does not carry it. The fallback for "no pin" is 0, and 0 is
the value that means MINT A NEW TREE — so bringing the stack up from a worktree
silently re-pointed Rekor at an empty log. Measured on 2026-09-16: 74 entries
became 1, and every commit ever signed answered `unavailable` for its inclusion
proof.

Nothing was destroyed. The old tree was still in the log database, which
bring-up never recreates, and restoring the pin brought all 74 back with the
same commit verifying at the same index. Recoverable is not the same as safe:
the failure is silent, it presents as every historical commit going
unverifiable at once, and the obvious reading of that is that the log is gone.

Two rules, and the second is the one that matters.

The pin now resolves against the REPOSITORY rather than whichever tree the
command ran from, reusing the rule RM-148 already established for the link step
— one answer to "which tree is the repository", in one place.

And minting is REFUSED when a log already exists. Rule one fixes the case that
was hit and leaves the class: any way of losing the pin — a `git clean -x`, a
clone beside an existing deployment, a restored home directory — puts 0 back in
front of a live log. A pin is a cache of a fact the deployment already holds, so
minting over it is only ever right when there is no log yet, and that is
checkable. The refusal names the repository that has the pin, the command that
recovers the id from a running log, and the explicit opt-in for the case where
minting really is what was meant.

The selftest builds a REAL git worktree, because the bug is not in the pin's
content but in which file is read: a fixture that only wrote and read a file
would pass against the broken code, which also read a file — the wrong one. It
also asserts that a worktree genuinely lacks the gitignored pin, so the case
cannot quietly stop being a case, and that a machine with no log may still mint,
or the guard would refuse every first boot.

Mutation-tested by restoring the old rule: three cases go red, including the one
that would have returned 0 and minted.

